# Firebase & Firestore Patterns

## Firestore Structure

- Use subcollections: `users/{userId}/notes/{noteId}`
- Store user-specific data under user document
- Use proper Firestore types (Timestamp, etc.)
- Implement proper security rules
- Handle offline capabilities

## Service Layer Pattern

- Create service functions in `/services` directory
- Use TanStack Query mutations/queries (not direct Firebase calls in components)
- Return typed data from service functions
- Handle errors at service level
- Use Firebase v9+ modular SDK

## Example Service Function

```typescript
import { Timestamp, addDoc, collection } from 'firebase/firestore'

import { db } from '@/lib/firebase'

export interface Note {
  id: string
  title: string
  content: string
  createdAt: Timestamp
  updatedAt: Timestamp
  userId: string
}

export async function createNote(
  userId: string,
  noteData: Omit<Note, 'id' | 'createdAt' | 'updatedAt'>
): Promise<Note> {
  const notesRef = collection(db, `users/${userId}/notes`)
  const newNote = {
    ...noteData,
    createdAt: Timestamp.now(),
    updatedAt: Timestamp.now(),
  }
  const docRef = await addDoc(notesRef, newNote)
  return { id: docRef.id, ...newNote }
}
```

## TanStack Query Integration

- Use `useQuery` for fetching data
- Use `useMutation` for create/update/delete operations
- Use query keys: `['notes', userId]` for caching
- Invalidate queries after mutations
- Handle loading and error states

## Firebase Storage

- Use Firebase Storage for images/files
- Upload files before saving note references
- Use proper file paths: `users/{userId}/notes/{noteId}/images/{imageId}`
- Handle upload progress and errors
